'''General Carrera Lake''' (Chilean part, officially renamed in 1959)<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.leychile.cl/Navegar?idNorma=27455&idVersion=1959-09-09|title=Ley N.º 13.375: Crea y fija los limites de los departamentos de Palena, Aysén, Coyhaique y Chile Chico y las de sus respectivas comunas-subdelegaciones|publisher=Ministerio del Interior de Chile|date=1959-09-09}}</ref> or '''Lake Buenos Aires''' (Argentine part) is a deep [[lake]] located in [[Patagonia]] and shared by [[Argentina]] and [[Chile]]. Both names are internationally accepted, while the [[Indigenous language|autochthonous]] name of the lake is '''Chelenko''', which means "stormy waters" in [[Tehuelche language|Aonikenk]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Prudencio Vergara|first=Guillermo|date=2019-05-06|title=Una "fiebre del oro" junto al mayor lago de la Patagonia|trans-title=A "gold fever" along the major lake in Patagonia|url=https://elpais.com/elpais/2019/03/25/planeta_futuro/1553527543_953319.html|language=es|work=El País|location=Chile Chico|access-date=2021-12-29}}</ref> Another historical name is '''Coluguape''' from [[Mapuche language|Mapuche]], a derivative of this name is applied to [[Lake Colhué Huapi|Colhué Huapí Lake]] after Argentine explorer [[Francisco Moreno]] reached this lake in 1876 conflating it with Coluguape (General Carrera Lake).<ref name=Coluguape>{{Cite journal |title=Identificación del lago "Coluguape" en la cartografía histórica (1775-1898): Su vinculación con el lago Buenos aires-General Carrera y el sistema lacustre Musters-Colhue Huapi |journal=[[Magallania]] |last1=Melo |first1=Walter D. |issue=1 |volume=45 |last2=Scordo |first2=Facundo |doi=10.4067/S0718-22442017000100015 |year=2017 |pages=15–33 |language=Spanish |trans-title=Identification of the "Coluguape" lake in historical cartography (1775-1898): Its connection to the Buenos Aires-General Carrera lake and Musters- Colhue Huapi lake system |last3=Perillo |first3=Gerardo M. E. |last4=Píccolo |first4=M. Cintia|doi-access=free |hdl=11336/27464 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>

The lake is of [[glacial]] origin and is surrounded by the [[Andes]] mountain range. The lake drains to the [[Pacific Ocean]] on the west through the [[Baker River (Chile)|Baker River]]. During the [[Llanquihue glaciation|last glaciation]] the lake drained to the Atlantic through [[Deseado River]].<ref name=Coluguape/>

The weather in this area of Chile and Argentina is generally cold and humid. But the lake itself has a sunny [[microclimate]], a weather pattern enjoyed by the few settlements along the lake, such as [[Puerto Guadal]], [[Fachinal]], Mallín Grande, [[Puerto Murta]], Puerto Río Tranquilo, [[Puerto Sánchez]], [[Puerto Ingeniero Ibáñez]] and [[Chile Chico]] in Chile, and [[Los Antiguos]] and [[Perito Moreno, Santa Cruz|Perito Moreno]] in Argentina.

The area near the coast of the lake was first inhabited by [[Creole peoples|criollos]] and [[Demographics of Europe|European]] immigrants between 1900 and 1925. In 1971 and 1991, eruptions of the [[Mount Hudson|Hudson Volcano]] severely affected the local economy, especially that of [[sheep]] farming. A car ferry operates between Puerto Ingeniero Ibáñez and Chile Chico in the Chilean sector of the lake. The lake is known as a [[trout]] and [[salmon]] fishing destination.

== Area ==
The lake has a surface of {{cvt|1,850|km²}} of which {{convert|970|km²}} are in the Chilean [[Aysén del General Carlos Ibáñez del Campo Region]], and {{convert|880|km²}} in the Argentine [[Santa Cruz Province (Argentina)|Santa Cruz Province]], making it the biggest lake in Chile, and the fourth largest in Argentina. In its western basin, Lake Gen. Carrera has {{convert|586|m|ft|abbr=on}} maximum depth.<ref>Murdie et al. 1999, Geo-Marine Letters 18:315-320.</ref>

== Geology ==
{{see also|Los Domos}}
The lake occupies a continental-scale [[graben]] formed by SWS-ENE normal faults that have resulted in down-dropping the bottom of the lake to {{convert|350|m|sp=us}} below mean sea level.<ref>B. Scalabrino, et al., A morphotectonic analysis of central Patagonian Cordillera: Negative inversion of the Andean belt over a buried spreading center?, TECTONICS, VOL. 29, TC2010, doi:10.1029/2009TC002453, 2010</ref> Preservation of younger lithostratigraphic units within the graben form reverse stratigraphy with older units exposed at higher topographic elevations to the south. The graben channeled mountain glaciers which formed terminal moraine helping to modify the present-day shape of the lake. The tectonic activity that formed the depression can be inferred to subduction of the triple joint that has occurred over the past 20 million years, as indicated by ripple marks in volcaniclastic sediments observed along the southern shoreline. There is some speculation on whether the tectonics and [[crustal heat flow]] in the lake area are influenced by the [[Slab window|asthenospheric window]] that exists beneath the crust in this region of Patagonia.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Lagabrielle |first1=Yves |last2=Suárez |first2=Manuel |last3=Rossello |first3=Eduardo A.|last4=Hérail |first4=Gérard |last5=Martinod |first5=Joseph|last6=Régnier |first6=Marc |last7=de la Cruz |first7=Rita |date=2004 |title=Neogene to Quaternary tectonic evolution of the Patagonian Andes at the latitude of the Chile Triple Junction |journal=Tectonophysics |volume=385 |issue= 1–4|pages=211–241 |doi= 10.1016/j.tecto.2004.04.023 |bibcode=2004Tectp.385..211L }}</ref>

The Marble Caves, Marble Chapel, and Marble Cathedral are unusual geological formations located on the shoreline midway along the lake's length. They represent a group of caverns, columns, and tunnels formed in monoliths of marble. The Marble Caves have been formed by wave action over the last 6,200 years.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.wondermondo.com/Countries/SA/Chile/Aisen/CapillaDelMarmol.htm|title=The Marble Caves (Cavernas de Mármol)|publisher=Wondermondo|date=2012-01-10|access-date=2012-01-10|archive-date=2017-11-13|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171113042546/http://www.wondermondo.com/Countries/SA/Chile/Aisen/CapillaDelMarmol.htm|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The northwestern shores have valuable mineral deposits of [[lead]] and [[zinc]] which were exploited intermittently from 1931 to 1996 in Puerto Cristal.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Campamento Minero Puerto Cristal. |url=https://www.monumentos.gob.cl/monumentos/monumentos-historicos/campamento-minero-puerto-cristal |access-date=2025-04-29 |website=monumentos.gob.cl |publisher=[[Consejo de Monumentos Nacionales de Chile]] |language=es}}</ref>

There are various [[river delta]]s on the lake's shores and some of them have older parts that are raised showing ivendence of higher lake levels in the past. The three main deltas lie at the outlet of the rivers Maitenes, Aviles and [[Jeinimeni River|Jeinimeni]] on the southern shores.<ref>Williams. 2022. [https://www.scielo.cl/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0718-71062022000200147 Delta size variability at Lake General Carrera, Patagonia]. ''[[Andean Geology]]''.</ref>

